## Why This Is Asked
Tests for feature flags cover a practical risk area: runtime configuration. It invites candidates to reason about defaults, resilience, caching, and cross-environment consistency.

## Key Concepts
- Feature flags semantics and defaults
- Client-side vs server-side flag resolution
- Resilience to flag service outages and cache TTL
- End-to-end testing of dynamic UI state

## Code Example
```javascript
// Jest example for flag default
import {FlagClient} from './flags';
test('default off when offline', ()=> {
  const fc = new FlagClient({online:false});
  expect(fc.isOn('newHomepage')).toBe(false);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test TTL expiration and cache invalidation?
- How would you simulate a partial outage where some flags are reachable and others are not?

## Why This Is Asked

Evaluates the ability to design comprehensive yet accessible test strategies that balance data privacy protection with thorough API validation without compromising real user information.

## Key Concepts

- Synthetic data generation with seedable randomness
- Contract-based API testing and schema validation
- Input sanitization and malformed request handling
- Privacy constraint verification and data masking
- CI/CD integration for automated test pipelines

## Code Example

```javascript
// Seedable synthetic data generator
function generateProfile(seed) {
  const faker = new Faker({ seed });
  return {
    id: faker.uuid(),
    name: faker.name.fullName(),
    email: faker.internet.email()
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt test strategies for GDPR vs CCPA compliance requirements?
